'\" t
.TH DVBTX-POWER 8 2026-11-08 "dvbtx 0.3.0" "dvbtx manual"
.SH NAME
dvbtx\-power \- power profiles of the machine for a transmitter on battery: every saving while nothing is on air, a transmit profile while dvbtx runs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY "dvbtx power"
.BR save | transmit | default | status
.RB [ \-c
.IR FILE ]
.YS
.SY "dvbtx power hook"
.BR start | stop
.RI [ UNIT ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR FILE ]
.YS
.SY "systemctl enable \-\-now dvbtx\-power"
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
A transmitter carried with its radios in a bag runs on a battery: every watt saved is autonomy,
and less heat.
.B dvbtx power
switches the whole machine between two profiles:
.TP
.B save
Nothing is on air: every saving the hardware offers.
Applied at boot, and when the last dvbtx unit stops (also after a crash).
.TP
.B transmit
A dvbtx unit runs: what the real-time work of the radios needs, the other savings kept.
Applied by each dvbtx unit as it starts.
.PP
It is
.BR opt-in :
nothing changes until
.B dvbtx\-power.service
is enabled.
The settings apply to the whole machine, including other programs and virtual machines running
on it.
.SS What each profile sets
.TS
allbox tab(@);
lb lb lb
l l l.
setting@save@transmit
cpufreq governor@powersave@powersave
energy/performance preference (EPP)@power@balance_power
turbo / boost@off@allowed
PCIe ASPM policy@powersupersave@default (firmware)
USB autosuspend@on, keyboards and mice spared@off: radios never suspended
PCI runtime power management@auto@auto
SATA link power (ALPM)@med_power_with_dipm@med_power_with_dipm
HDA / AC97 audio power save@on@on
NMI watchdog@off@off
dirty page writeback@15 s@15 s
Wi-Fi power save (iw)@on@on
.TE
.PP
Each value is written only where the machine offers it: an AMD or older Intel processor without
EPP keeps its governor setting only, a firmware that keeps PCIe ASPM to itself (ACPI FADT) refuses
the policy, which is then left as it is.
.PP
In transmit, USB autosuspend is off because a radio whose multiplexes are all stopped is closed and
opened again later: it must not be suspended meanwhile.
The PCIe policy goes back to the firmware's so that a USB controller behind a PCIe link answers
the radio's DMA without delay.
.SS Processor idle states
The engine also holds
.I /dev/cpu_dma_latency
while it runs
.RB ( "[engine] cpu_latency_us" ,
see
.BR dvbtx\-engine (8)
and
.BR dvbtx.toml (5)).
That request is system wide too, and lasts exactly as long as the engine: the kernel drops it when
the engine exits, even after a crash.
0 keeps every core awake (no idle state at all: the most power).
100 lets the cores sleep down to C6 (85 \(mcs to wake up on an Intel 8th generation) and forbids
only the deepest states (C7 to C10, 124 to 890 \(mcs), whose exit delays the USB DMA long enough
to empty the HackRF's 1 ms FIFO: gaps on air.
That holds only while the processor is busy: on the same i3 with the HackRF alone at 19.6 MS/s
(the B210 unplugged, the processor mostly idle), 100 gave about 150 gaps a second, 0 none.
Keep the default 0 unless you have measured another value with your radios and your load.
\-1 makes no request.
.SS Measured
Intel Core i3-8100T, four multiplexes on a HackRF and a B210 (DVB-T, DVB-T2, DVB-C), processor
package power read from RAPL:
.TS
allbox tab(@);
lb lb lb
l r l.
setup@power@on air
idle, performance governor@1.37 W@\(em
idle, save@0.62 W@\(em
on air, cpu_latency_us 0, performance governor@22.1 W@no underrun
on air, cpu_latency_us 100, transmit@13.9 W@no underrun, margins unchanged
on air, cpu_latency_us 100, EPP power@14.2 W@no underrun
.TE
.PP
The EPP
.B power
brings nothing while the processor works at 75 %, hence
.B balance_power
for transmit.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B save
Apply the save profile now.
.TP
.B transmit
Apply the transmit profile now.
.TP
.B default
Save, or transmit when a dvbtx unit is on air.
This is what
.B dvbtx\-power.service
runs at boot (ordered before the dvbtx units).
.TP
.B status
The profile last applied, the dvbtx units on air, whether
.B dvbtx\-power.service
is enabled, the governor, EPP, turbo, ASPM policy, the USB devices by power control, and the
processor package power over 2 seconds (RAPL, root only).
.TP
.BR "hook start" | stop " [\fIUNIT\fR]"
Called by the dvbtx units as root
.RB ( ExecStartPre=
and
.BR ExecStopPost= ).
.B start
marks the unit as on air and applies transmit;
.B stop
removes the mark and applies save when no other unit is on air.
Does nothing unless
.B dvbtx\-power.service
is enabled.
.PP
Every command but
.B status
needs root.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c " FILE" "\fR, \fP\-\-config " FILE
Configuration file whose
.B [power]
table is read (default
.IR /etc/dvbtx/dvbtx.toml ).
A file that cannot be read gives the default settings, with a warning: a hook never fails.
.SH CONFIGURATION
In the
.B [power]
table of
.BR dvbtx.toml (5);
a change is used at the next switch of profile (or at once with
.BR "dvbtx power transmit" ),
it does not restart the transmission.
.TP
.BR save_epp " (\(dqpower\(dq), " transmit_epp " (\(dqbalance_power\(dq)"
Energy/performance preference:
.BR performance ,
.BR balance_performance ,
.BR balance_power ,
.BR power .
.TP
.BR save_turbo " (false), " transmit_turbo " (true)"
Turbo or boost allowed.
.TP
.BR save_aspm " (\(dqpowersupersave\(dq), " transmit_aspm " (\(dqdefault\(dq)"
PCIe ASPM policy:
.BR default ,
.BR performance ,
.BR powersave ,
.BR powersupersave
(powersave when powersupersave is refused).
.TP
.BR save_command ", " transmit_command " (\(dq\(dq)"
Shell command run after the profile, e.g. to dim a backlight or slow a fan.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/dvbtx\-power.service
Oneshot unit, enabled by the administrator: runs
.B dvbtx power default
at boot.
.TP
.IR /usr/lib/systemd/system/dvbtx.service ", " dvbtx@.service
Call
.B dvbtx power hook start|stop
.BR %n .
.TP
.I /run/dvbtx\-power/profile
The profile last applied.
.TP
.IR /run/dvbtx\-power/on/ UNIT
One file per dvbtx unit on air.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
sudo systemctl enable \-\-now dvbtx\-power     # save now and at every boot
sudo dvbtx power status
sudo systemctl start dvbtx                   # transmit
sudo systemctl stop dvbtx                    # save again
.EE
.PP
From a source checkout, the same with
.BR "sudo packaging/install\-service.sh \-c FILE \-\-power" .
.PP
Never let the processor speed up, even on air:
.PP
.EX
[power]
transmit_turbo = false
.EE
